A||N||7||d825077||fuel filter||||1||
Z||000000||d825077||07-29-2000||10:36 PM||||How often should I change my fuel filter on my 84 fireo se.  It is automatic with 77,000 miles on it.  Are there any signs like lose of power to change the filter?  With that many miles on it do you think I should change it?  How hard is it and will I see an improvement in idling and power if I replace it.  My 88 fiero had a problem and my machanic changed the filter and it runs better than when I bought it.  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||07-29-2000||11:38 PM||||They're cheap and simple enough that if you can't remember when you changed it last, it's probably time again...<BR>I change mine about once a year or so, when the mood hits me, or when I'm at the parts store or Wally World and I see one on the shelf.  Any less frequent and the fittings will probably rust and I'll never get it off.  And I don't have to deal with salted roads.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||07-30-2000||02:15 AM||||Exactly....besides, if memory serves, I believe that on the 4 banger Fieros, the fuel filter is right up top, way easy to get to. Unlike those of us w/ the 6 cylinders who have to jack up the car and slide under. Im actually glad you brought this up, it reminds me that I need to change mine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-30-2000||11:19 PM||||It's on top on the older 4's. It's under on 87+up 4's same as a 6.<P>The Fiero filter is so large it lasts for years in "normal use." Can't hurt to change it every year or 3. Especially if the fuel in you area is often dirty. I think mine is still the original one. (I intend to change it along with the list of 100+ other things I need to do.)<P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||07-30-2000||11:24 PM||||I would change it soon. The fuel filter is something I neglect also, as gas stations have pretty good filteration systems. I bought an 87GT a year ago, started having performance problems, so I started looking at tune up stuff like the filter. Ended up being primarily the cat., but the fuel filter was 80% plugged so it probably contributed. When I removed it, I could barely blow through it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||d825077||08-01-2000||10:09 PM||||Today I finally changed the filter.  It was the easiest thing I ever did for my car.  When I empetied the filter the gas that came out had a brown tint.  Is this normal?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||08-02-2000||08:55 AM||||The brown tint just means that the filter was doing its job.  That is the trash it captured from the fuel tank.||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||08-02-2000||02:43 PM||||Sounds like rust in the fuel tank. When you take the tank out to change the fuel pump and screen/filter or sensor, use a  fuel tank sealer. I got mine from an east coast co, may be NJ. I found him in Hemmings News. They advertise they are the best. They work and are the only one I have used.<P>Basically you put a bike chain in the tank and slide it around by shaking it (2 person job). This removes the big chunks. If you really want to get anal you put in an anti- rust compound, (hardware store paint dept) which is phosphoric acid. This converts iron oxide to iron phosphate. Ending the continuing rust problem. Then add the gas tank sealler, and slosh it around. It forms a hard coating that gas/alcohol/h2o won't eat through. I've ended rust problems on 2 cars this way, it works.||||reg||1
